WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:03.680
- [Narrator] In 1966, a
disaster scarred a village

00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:04.693
in South Wales.

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:09.130
What happened was unthinkable.

00:00:09.130 --> 00:00:11.340
A coal tip on a mountain
slope above the village

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:14.380
of Alba Van collapsed, crashing directly

00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:15.923
into a local school below it.

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:26.260
In an instant, much of a
village's generation was lost.

00:00:26.260 --> 00:00:28.550
Many of those who
survived were traumatized

00:00:28.550 --> 00:00:31.250
for the rest of their lives,
but what does this tragedy have

00:00:31.250 --> 00:00:32.760
to do with Queen Elizabeth?

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:34.010
That's what episode three

00:00:34.010 --> 00:00:36.010
of The Crown's third season deals with

00:00:36.010 --> 00:00:38.550
as we see Queen Elizabeth
grapple with what it means

00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:40.680
to be the stoic figurehead of a nation

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:42.773
in the face of unimaginable tragedy.

00:00:43.690 --> 00:00:46.070
It also shows how the
events which followed

00:00:46.070 --> 00:00:48.290
reportedly became one
of the greatest regrets

00:00:48.290 --> 00:00:49.703
of Queen Elizabeth's reign.

00:00:51.039 --> 00:00:53.470
In this video, we're
going to tell you more

00:00:53.470 --> 00:00:56.320
about what happened
some of the reasons why

00:00:56.320 --> 00:00:57.823
and what happened afterwards.

00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:05.860
As with everything in The Crown,

00:01:05.860 --> 00:01:09.480
the episode was meticulously
researched, but it is a drama,

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:11.400
so some creative license is used

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:13.023
to drive the personal stories.

00:01:17.180 --> 00:01:20.470
Class is a recurring theme
in this series of The Crown,

00:01:20.470 --> 00:01:23.310
but it's never more pertinent
than in this episode.

00:01:23.310 --> 00:01:25.670
So to understand the story behind it,

00:01:25.670 --> 00:01:28.590
you also need to understand
the history of mining towns

00:01:28.590 --> 00:01:30.323
and villages in South Wales.

00:01:31.225 --> 00:01:34.920
From the 19th century until
the early 20th century,

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:37.190
coal mining was crucial to Wales and parts

00:01:37.190 --> 00:01:38.890
of the north of England.

00:01:38.890 --> 00:01:41.980
Mines provided jobs which
in turn created pockets

00:01:41.980 --> 00:01:44.750
of mining communities
spreading across areas

00:01:44.750 --> 00:01:46.560
like the South Wales Valleys.

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:49.550
By the 1960s, mining
was beginning to dwindle

00:01:49.550 --> 00:01:51.350
due to competition from oil,

00:01:51.350 --> 00:01:54.400
yet coal mining still
fuelled the community of

00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:57.690
not just Alba Van, but
neighboring villages too.

00:01:57.690 --> 00:02:00.830
Think of the mine as the
central hub of this village.

00:02:00.830 --> 00:02:02.850
If no one in your family worked there,

00:02:02.850 --> 00:02:05.180
someone you knew definitely did.

00:02:05.180 --> 00:02:08.570
It was dangerous work, but
people assumed it was dangerous

00:02:08.570 --> 00:02:10.733
for miners, not their families.

00:02:12.100 --> 00:02:14.740
Surrounding these mines
were huge tips built

00:02:14.740 --> 00:02:17.360
from waste produced
during the mining process.

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:19.050
The tips were known to slip.

00:02:19.050 --> 00:02:22.890
In fact, there were three
previous significant tip slides,

00:02:22.890 --> 00:02:26.673
but none to the extent tip
number seven did in 1966.

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:31.070
That tip in particular
had concerned the people

00:02:31.070 --> 00:02:34.010
of Alba Van for years
because it was located

00:02:34.010 --> 00:02:37.080
almost directly above
Pantglas Junior School,

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:38.143
the local primary.

00:02:38.996 --> 00:02:41.572
What's more, it was
discovered the coal waste lay

00:02:41.572 --> 00:02:42.830
on top of a mountain spring

00:02:42.830 --> 00:02:45.990
which meant the whole thing
was dangerously fluid.

00:02:45.990 --> 00:02:49.250
At the time, the mines were
run by the National Coal Board.

00:02:49.250 --> 00:02:52.590
A statutory corporation set
up by the British government

00:02:52.590 --> 00:02:55.330
when mines were nationalized in 1947.

00:02:55.330 --> 00:02:57.400
Three years before the disaster,

00:02:57.400 --> 00:02:59.750
a waterworks engineer
wrote to the coal board

00:02:59.750 --> 00:03:02.350
about the danger that it posed.

00:03:02.350 --> 00:03:05.320
Local councillors also
warned of the danger.

00:03:05.320 --> 00:03:08.060
Parents from Pantglas
school signed a petition,

00:03:08.060 --> 00:03:09.200
they were ignored.

00:03:10.186 --> 00:03:11.019
- [Man] We've been
telling everyone for years

00:03:11.019 --> 00:03:11.933
those tips were dangerous.

00:03:15.745 --> 00:03:17.700
- [Narrator] Here's
what happened that day.

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:20.230
For weeks beforehand, the
Mirtha Valley had been hit

00:03:20.230 --> 00:03:24.470
by a torrential downpour,
turning the tip to slurry.

00:03:24.470 --> 00:03:25.780
Workers arrived in the morning

00:03:25.780 --> 00:03:29.363
to find it had sunk
dangerously by about 10 feet.

00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:33.040
There were no phone lines
working up there that day,

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:35.310
so workers hurried their
way down the mountain

00:03:35.310 --> 00:03:37.796
to warn bosses of the danger.

00:03:37.796 --> 00:03:40.546
(people arguing)

00:03:41.850 --> 00:03:42.683
But it was too late.

00:03:44.750 --> 00:03:48.147
The tip began to fall at
approximately 9:15 a.m.,

00:03:49.630 --> 00:03:52.020
just as children had
sat down on the morning

00:03:52.020 --> 00:03:54.970
of what was the last day before half-term.

00:03:54.970 --> 00:03:58.180
If the tip had fallen
just 20 minutes earlier,

00:03:58.180 --> 00:04:00.990
the story may well have
been entirely different.

00:04:00.990 --> 00:04:03.880
The teachers had just begun
taking their attendance

00:04:03.880 --> 00:04:05.230
when the landslide hit.

00:04:05.230 --> 00:04:08.720
An avalanche of liquefied
slurry tore down the mountain,

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:11.600
reaching speeds of 30
to 40 miles per hour.

00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:13.550
Survivors who are inside the school

00:04:13.550 --> 00:04:16.880
described hearing a thunderous
noise like a jet plane

00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:19.010
as the avalanche approached.

00:04:19.010 --> 00:04:21.080
As had long been feared by those

00:04:21.080 --> 00:04:23.200
who had warned the National Coal Board,

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:25.340
the mud crashed directly into the school

00:04:25.340 --> 00:04:27.414
and many of the neighboring houses.

00:04:27.414 --> 00:04:31.663
It happened so quickly, there
was barely time to react.

00:04:35.550 --> 00:04:37.920
As soon as news of the tragedy spread,

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:40.843
people from across South Wales
arrived Alba Van to help.

00:04:41.983 --> 00:04:44.673
Miners found themselves
digging for their own children.

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:50.180
Initially, they worried about bringing

00:04:50.180 --> 00:04:51.453
in mechanical diggers,

00:04:52.699 --> 00:04:55.000
fearing their weight would
crush anyone trapped underneath,

00:04:55.000 --> 00:04:58.100
so many began digging
with their bare hands.

00:04:58.100 --> 00:04:59.740
Whenever anyone in the rescue efforts

00:04:59.740 --> 00:05:02.710
thought they heard someone
buried under the heap of dirt,

00:05:02.710 --> 00:05:05.853
a whistle was blown, stopping
everyone in their tracks.

00:05:08.750 --> 00:05:11.417
(whistle blown)

00:05:15.750 --> 00:05:18.350
While some children were pulled out alive,

00:05:18.350 --> 00:05:21.200
the rescue effort was
unimaginably difficult

00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:24.040
due to the sheer amount
of hardened slurry.

00:05:24.040 --> 00:05:29.040
In the end, 116 children and
28 adults were found dead

00:05:29.110 --> 00:05:30.930
and it took a week to find them all

00:05:30.930 --> 00:05:32.980
because there was so much to dig through.

00:05:34.450 --> 00:05:36.020
As shown in the episode,

00:05:36.020 --> 00:05:38.633
the local Chapel became
a makeshift mortuary.

00:05:40.980 --> 00:05:44.220
In the show, Queen Elizabeth
eventually visits Alba Van

00:05:44.220 --> 00:05:46.630
following the threat of
criticism from the press.

00:05:46.630 --> 00:05:50.030
- The very last thing Emergency
and Rescue Services need

00:05:50.030 --> 00:05:53.090
when they're working against
the clock is a Queen to arrive.

00:05:53.090 --> 00:05:54.660
- [Narrator] In real life, it did indeed

00:05:54.660 --> 00:05:57.210
take the Queen eight
days to visit Alba Van.

00:05:57.210 --> 00:06:00.520
Later in 2002, it was reported the Queen

00:06:00.520 --> 00:06:03.230
said her biggest regret
was not visiting Alba Van

00:06:03.230 --> 00:06:05.270
immediately after the disaster.

00:06:05.270 --> 00:06:08.150
Buckingham Palace has never
confirmed nor denied this,

00:06:08.150 --> 00:06:10.520
but the Queen, out of all the Royals,

00:06:10.520 --> 00:06:12.333
has returned to Alba Van the most.

00:06:16.540 --> 00:06:19.453
What you don't see in the
episode is what happened later.

00:06:20.560 --> 00:06:22.320
PTSD was common in those children

00:06:22.320 --> 00:06:23.910
who did survive the disaster.

00:06:23.910 --> 00:06:26.410
Many of them suffering
from survivor's guilt

00:06:26.410 --> 00:06:28.370
as they attempted to come to terms both

00:06:28.370 --> 00:06:29.930
with the death of their friends

00:06:29.930 --> 00:06:31.810
and with their own personal trauma.

00:06:31.810 --> 00:06:34.740
Miners too, felt overwhelming guilt,

00:06:34.740 --> 00:06:37.050
as did parents who wish
they'd stop their children

00:06:37.050 --> 00:06:38.550
from going to school that day.

00:06:39.790 --> 00:06:41.160
Bereaved parents struggled

00:06:42.370 --> 00:06:43.600
with even seeing other
children out in the streets.

00:06:43.600 --> 00:06:45.970
The entire village was in mourning.

00:06:45.970 --> 00:06:47.870
The children who died became known

00:06:47.870 --> 00:06:50.280
as Alba Van lost generation.

00:06:50.280 --> 00:06:52.340
We see a taste of their grief and anger

00:06:52.340 --> 00:06:55.230
towards the National Coal
Board in the episode.

00:06:55.230 --> 00:06:57.951
- Will you both accept responsibility?

00:06:57.951 --> 00:06:59.170
(crowd shouting)

00:06:59.170 --> 00:07:00.330
- [Narrator] This anger spilled

00:07:00.330 --> 00:07:02.980
into the official inquest into the deaths.

00:07:02.980 --> 00:07:04.360
One father shouted.

00:07:04.360 --> 00:07:08.020
- Buried alive by the National Coal Board.

00:07:08.020 --> 00:07:10.670
That's what I want to see
written on my child's desk.

00:07:11.517 --> 00:07:13.720
- [Narrator] Another person
who didn't go to the site

00:07:13.720 --> 00:07:16.270
of the disaster immediately
was the chairman

00:07:16.270 --> 00:07:19.000
of the National Coal Board, Lord Robins.

00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:20.520
Preferring instead to continue

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:23.010
with a ceremony installing
him as the Chancellor

00:07:23.010 --> 00:07:24.660
of the University of Surrey.

00:07:24.660 --> 00:07:27.580
The day after his eventual
visit, he was interviewed

00:07:27.580 --> 00:07:30.070
by a television news team claiming no one

00:07:30.070 --> 00:07:32.860
from the board knew there was
a spring underneath the tip.

00:07:32.860 --> 00:07:35.300
The NCB resisted taking the blame,

00:07:35.300 --> 00:07:37.540
claiming the disaster was
down to the heavy rain

00:07:37.540 --> 00:07:39.980
and other geological factors.

00:07:39.980 --> 00:07:42.320
- National Coal Board
cannot accept responsibility

00:07:42.320 --> 00:07:43.390
for the weather.

00:07:43.390 --> 00:07:45.450
- [Narrator] Ultimately, a
tribunal placed the blame

00:07:45.450 --> 00:07:48.260
for the disaster entirely with the NCB,

00:07:48.260 --> 00:07:50.700
criticizing a complete lack of policy

00:07:50.700 --> 00:07:53.284
when it came to dealing with the tips.

00:07:53.284 --> 00:07:56.284
(light eerie music)

00:08:06.400 --> 00:08:09.160
It was the largest
tribunal in British history

00:08:09.160 --> 00:08:14.160
with around 130 witnesses,
yet no one was prosecuted

00:08:14.340 --> 00:08:16.990
and no one from the board
lost their job as a result.

00:08:18.110 --> 00:08:21.320
Lord Robins publicly
offered his resignation.

00:08:21.320 --> 00:08:23.861
- But I felt the circumstances were such

00:08:23.861 --> 00:08:26.440
that my resignation should
be offered and therefore,

00:08:26.440 --> 00:08:28.330
it's really a matter for
the government to decide

00:08:28.330 --> 00:08:30.690
whether they accept my resignation.

00:08:30.690 --> 00:08:32.440
- [Narrator] But papers
released decades later

00:08:32.440 --> 00:08:34.300
revealed he had only done so

00:08:34.300 --> 00:08:36.780
when he was assured his job was safe.

00:08:36.780 --> 00:08:39.120
Not only did he remain in his post,

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:41.110
he was appointed to chair a committee

00:08:41.110 --> 00:08:43.070
which made recommendations about health

00:08:43.070 --> 00:08:45.270
and safety regulations.

00:08:45.270 --> 00:08:47.280
In the years since the disaster,

00:08:47.280 --> 00:08:50.760
it also emerged how badly the
victims families were treated

00:08:50.760 --> 00:08:53.684
as the NCB attempted to
dodge responsibility.

00:08:53.684 --> 00:08:56.880
A study from 2000 from the
board and the government

00:08:56.880 --> 00:08:59.330
of the day were extremely insensitive

00:08:59.330 --> 00:09:00.940
to the victims' families.

00:09:00.940 --> 00:09:03.970
Dr. Ian Machlin, one of
the authors of the report

00:09:03.970 --> 00:09:07.170
said victims families were
treated as troublemakers

00:09:07.170 --> 00:09:09.770
and there was further hurt
still for those families.

00:09:10.930 --> 00:09:14.100
A disaster fund set up by
the mayor of Merthyr Tydfil

00:09:14.100 --> 00:09:16.530
had drawn donations
from all over the world

00:09:16.530 --> 00:09:20.230
with a final total of around 1.75 million.

00:09:20.230 --> 00:09:22.780
As legal battles over the fund continued,

00:09:22.780 --> 00:09:24.820
residents from the village
had begun a campaign

00:09:24.820 --> 00:09:27.080
to remove the tips surrounding Alba Van,

00:09:27.080 --> 00:09:29.930
fearing they could collapse once again,

00:09:29.930 --> 00:09:32.970
but the NCB refused to take
financial responsibility

00:09:32.970 --> 00:09:34.965
for the removal of the tips.

00:09:34.965 --> 00:09:38.340
In the end, the government took a 150,000

00:09:38.340 --> 00:09:40.930
from the fund to make up the funds needed

00:09:40.930 --> 00:09:42.520
to get rid of the tips.

00:09:42.520 --> 00:09:45.120
It was yet another blow to the community,

00:09:45.120 --> 00:09:47.600
described by the press
as the second disaster

00:09:47.600 --> 00:09:49.470
to befall Alba Van.

00:09:49.470 --> 00:09:52.920
That money was only repaid in 1997,

00:09:52.920 --> 00:09:55.600
but without accounting for inflation.

00:09:55.600 --> 00:09:57.760
With inflation, that
amount would have been

00:09:57.760 --> 00:09:59.523
over a million pounds.

00:10:04.970 --> 00:10:07.950
The Crown's writer, Peter Morgan
said he had always planned

00:10:07.950 --> 00:10:09.840
to cover Alba Van in the series.

00:10:09.840 --> 00:10:11.810
The team behind the show
worked with the people

00:10:11.810 --> 00:10:14.130
of the village to ensure
the story was told

00:10:14.130 --> 00:10:16.740
with sensitivity, as well
as to bring the history

00:10:16.740 --> 00:10:18.933
to a wider, global audience.

00:10:19.810 --> 00:10:22.360
The community was involved
from the very beginning.

00:10:24.890 --> 00:10:27.230
Some people who were
relatives or neighbors

00:10:27.230 --> 00:10:29.300
of those who died in a Berlin appeared

00:10:29.300 --> 00:10:30.853
as extras in some scenes.

00:10:32.153 --> 00:10:34.200
(mumbles) Sowen, male voice
choir which was set up

00:10:34.200 --> 00:10:36.893
after the disaster sings in the episode.

00:10:38.159 --> 00:10:41.242
(male choir singing)

00:10:42.920 --> 00:10:45.800
The team decided not to
film in Alba Van itself,

00:10:45.800 --> 00:10:48.030
choosing nearby Kumamon instead.

00:10:48.030 --> 00:10:50.610
An empty school there became Pantglas,

00:10:50.610 --> 00:10:53.760
while the towering tip
was recreated in CGI.

00:10:53.760 --> 00:10:56.290
A rain machine recreated
the torrential downpour

00:10:56.290 --> 00:10:57.440
which fell on the valleys

00:10:57.440 --> 00:10:59.390
in the days leading up to the disaster.

00:11:00.683 --> 00:11:02.170
More than 50 years on,

00:11:02.170 --> 00:11:05.290
the trauma following the
disaster still remains

00:11:05.290 --> 00:11:07.370
as producers found when
they held a public meeting

00:11:07.370 --> 00:11:09.490
with people from the village.

00:11:09.490 --> 00:11:11.890
Everyone at that meeting, on set and

00:11:11.890 --> 00:11:13.900
at the subsequent screening
of the episode held

00:11:13.900 --> 00:11:16.023
in Alba Van were offered therapy.

00:11:17.270 --> 00:11:20.170
It's important to remember the
deep trauma Alba Van caused,

00:11:20.170 --> 00:11:23.340
not just to the area,
but to Wales as a whole

00:11:23.340 --> 00:11:25.520
as it became a symbol
of corporate negligence

00:11:25.520 --> 00:11:26.963
towards the working classes.

00:11:28.160 --> 00:11:30.510
Today, Alba Van is a very different place

00:11:30.510 --> 00:11:32.830
to the village we see in The Crown.

00:11:32.830 --> 00:11:35.690
The Mercer Vale (mumbles) closed in 1989,

00:11:35.690 --> 00:11:38.160
as have most of the mines in South Wales

00:11:38.160 --> 00:11:39.770
and the tips are long gone.

00:11:39.770 --> 00:11:42.313
The school has been replaced
by a memorial garden,

00:11:43.170 --> 00:11:45.310
but the village has never forgotten

00:11:45.310 --> 00:11:48.461
what happened there 53 years ago.

00:11:48.461 --> 00:11:51.878
(light electronic music)

